# Doctrine: Grace and Matter

This replaces the fasting exception in the active chooser. The original `theology` journey remains available directly with its original save identity. The new route is `doctrine-grace-and-matter-v1`.

The user's brief is a broad, continuing history of doctrinal issues, with alternating strands and earlier created ideas in every later chapter. Eight short chapters establish the first connected route; they do not exhaust that brief or Christian doctrine.

| Chapter | Question | Earlier creation used |
|---|---|---|
| Augustine: evil | Is evil a substance or a loss of good? | Opening chapter |
| Augustine: grace | Can instruction heal a damaged will? | Misdirected will |
| Medieval dualist text | Can one good first cause account for evil? | Evil as loss |
| Aquinas: reason | Does grace abolish or perfect nature? | Grace heals the will |
| Return to dualist ritual | Can bodily washing save a spirit? | An evil maker |
| Return to Aquinas | How could material signs convey grace? | Nature perfected |
| Luther, 1517 | What can an indulgence letter promise? | Instrument of grace |
| Luther, 1520 | If faith justifies, why do good works? | Grace heals the will |

The medieval chapters cross-cut concurrent strands. They do not assert that the ritual's community had read the Italian Book of Two Principles, that Aquinas answered that particular author, or that returning to an earlier Augustine token proves a specific transmission route. In contrast, Aquinas's citations of Augustine are explicit textual evidence of reuse. The Catholic sacramental account and Luther's indulgence challenge are connected as a comparison of claims about grace and assurance, not as an asserted direct derivation of the Theses from this passage of the Summa.

Primary texts are linked in the scenario's `sources`: Augustine's Enchiridion and On the Grace of Christ; the Book of Two Principles in CUHK's translated selection; the edited Occitan ritual; Summa I q.1 and III qq.60, 62, 75; Oxford's edition of the 95 Theses; and Luther's On Christian Liberty. Descriptions are attributed designer paraphrases. A recipe reconstructs the argument of a text; completion does not certify a belief as true. Theological positions should remain distinct, including the difference between Luther's 1517 and 1520 arguments.

The surviving dualist texts are particular witnesses. Do not generalise their combination into a universal Cathar creed, or infer a continuous Manichaean-to-Cathar institution from similar dualism. Augustine's anti-Pelagian writings are polemical evidence; this route reconstructs his positive account rather than treating his presentation as Pelagius's own voice.

Future extensions should add substantive opposing responses and earlier foundations (including Trinitarian and Christological disputes), and continue beyond Luther. Each extension needs documented connections, usable prior creations, and playtesting. Saints, Trinity, Christology, and later confessional settlements are not implemented by this version. The current two-combination chapters need human playtesting for depth and discoverability; route completion is functional validation only.
